The issue is the build.gradle .env parsing. It uses a basic line parser. Check if your .env file at 
.env
 has the correct content. The problem could be:

BOM characters — if the file was saved with UTF-8 BOM, the first variable gets corrupted
Windows line endings — the parser splits on = but the values might have trailing \r
Run this in PowerShell to verify what's actually in the file:

powershell

Get-Content "D:\workspace\ksp\KSP\backend\.env" | ForEach-Object { "[$_]" }
If the GROQ_API_KEY line shows correctly, the issue is likely the Gradle .env loader. Try passing it directly as an environment variable:

powershell

$env:GROQ_API_KEY="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"; .\gradlew.bat bootRun
If that works, then your .env file has an encoding issue. Recreate it with no BOM:

powershell

[System.IO.File]::WriteAllText("D:\workspace\ksp\KSP\backend\.env", "DB_URL=jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/postgres`nDB_USERNAME=postgres`nDB_PASSWORD=postgres`nJWT_SECRET=ksp-local-dev-secret-key-minimum-32-characters-long-for-hs256`nAI_PROVIDER=groq`nGROQ_API_KEY=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`nSPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=local`nEMAIL_ENABLED=false`n")
